Another symptom of the same root cause (the type cast does not give its operand a contextually expected type), this time for list and map constructors, at runtime. The cast compiles, but the constructor gets a fixed-length / closed inherent type instead of the cast type, so a later valid mutation panics with `inherent type violation`.

`cast-ctx-list-v.bal`:
```ballerina
import ballerina/io;

public function main() {
    // Spec 6.20: the contextually expected type for the operand of a
    // type-cast-expr is the intersection of the contextually expected type of
    // the cast and T, so the list constructor's inherent type is int[]
    // (growable), not a fixed-length tuple.
    int[] v = <int[]>[1];
    v.push(2);
    io:println(v); // @output [1,2]

    any a = <any[]>[1];
    if a is any[] {
        a.push("x");
        io:println(a); // @output [1,"x"]
    }
}
```
Actual:
```
error: inherent type violation
        at main(cast-ctx-list-v.bal:9)
```
Expected:
```
[1,2]
[1,"x"]
```

`cast-ctx-map-v.bal`:
```ballerina
import ballerina/io;

public function main() {
    // Same issue for mapping constructors: inherent type should be map<int>.
    map<int> m = <map<int>>{a: 1};
    m["b"] = 2;
    io:println(m); // @output {"a":1,"b":2}
}
```
Actual:
```
error: inherent type violation
        at main(cast-ctx-map-v.bal:6)
```
Expected: `{"a":1,"b":2}`

`int[] w = [1]; w.push(2);` works, and `var v = <int[]>[1]; v.push(2);` panics the same way, so the cast is the cause.

Spec §6.20 (type cast): "The contextually expected type for expression is the intersection of the contextually expected type of the type-cast-expr and the type described by the type-descriptor." §6.7.1: "If the applicable contextually expected type is a list type descriptor, then that used as the inherent type." §6.7.2 says the same for mapping type descriptors. So the inherent types should be `int[]`, `any[]` and `map<int>`, and `<anydata>[1, [2, 3]]` / `<anydata>{a: 1}` should also be growable.

